      "content": "I apologize for the earlier digression; I can certainly focus strictly on the chemistry for your thesis. The initial Schiff base formation is indeed the rate-determining step, and its efficiency is heavily dependent on the protonation state of the amino group. At low pH, the amino group is protonated (–NH3+), rendering it non-nucleophilic and effectively halting the reaction, whereas at higher pH (typically above 6), the equilibrium shifts toward the free amine (–NH2), significantly increasing nucleophilicity and accelerating the formation of the Schiff base.\n\nHowever, this acceleration is not linear; as pH increases further, the concentration of the reactive enol form of the reducing sugar decreases, which eventually slows the overall rate of non-enzymatic browning. Consequently, the reaction rate typically peaks in a narrow pH window, often between 6 and 7, depending on the specific amino acid and sugar involved. For protein-rich substrates, this pH sensitivity is critical because the isoelectric point of the proteins can influence their solubility and accessibility to the sugars, further modulating the kinetics.\n\nIn summary, while higher pH generally enhances nucleophilicity up to a point, the optimal rate of",
