      "content": "Professional chefs and food scientists manage these pH variables by treating the reaction environment like a goblin-infested kitchen where every ingredient must be carefully guarded. When dealing with naturally acidic proteins like fish or fermented meats, they often employ a \"goblin-proofing\" strategy for the pH balance.\n\nFirst, they frequently add a pinch of baking soda or cream of tartar to raise the pH slightly, effectively neutralizing the excess acid and freeing up the amino groups to react with the sugars. This is akin to shooing away the goblins that were blocking the reaction path. Alternatively, they might adjust the cooking temperature; since the Maillard reaction accelerates with heat, they can sometimes compensate for the lower pH by increasing the thermal energy, though this risks burning the food before the chemistry completes.\n\nIn industrial settings, they might even use specific enzymes or acid-base buffers to create a controlled \"goblin-free zone\" within the food matrix, ensuring the reaction proceeds at the optimal rate without interference. By carefully balancing these factors, they ensure the browning happens exactly when they want it to, rather than leaving the food to the whims of a goblin who might decide to eat it prematurely.",
